Article 1 (Purpose)
The purpose of this Rule is to prescribe matters delegated by the Act on Management of Firefighting Equipment and its Enforcement Decree and matters necessary for their enforcement.

Article 2 (Details of Implementation Results of City/Do Action Plans)
The details to be included in the implementation results of City/Do action plans on management of fire equipment (hereinafter referred to as “City/Do action plans”) under Article 4 (5) of the Enforcement Decree of the Act on Management of Firefighting Equipment (hereinafter referred to as the “Decree”) shall be as follows: <Amended on July 1, 2026>
1. Implementation results for each individual task in the City/Do action plan;
2. Problems in implementing the City/Do action plan and measures to address them;
3. Implementation results of education and training for operators of fire equipment;
4. The status and analysis of fire equipment accidents and preventive measures;
5. The operational status of the integrated management system of fire equipment under Article 40 of the Act on Management of Firefighting Equipment (hereinafter referred to as the “Act”);
6. Other results of policies implemented by each Special Metropolitan City, Integrated Special City, Metropolitan City, Special Self-Governing City, Do, or Special Self-Governing Province for the systematic management and operation of fire equipment.

Article 3 (Listing of Fire Equipment)
(1) Where the head of a fire service agency requests the Administrator of the Public Procurement Service to list fire equipment under Article 9 (1) of the Act, he or she shall provide the following data:
1. Identification data under Article 5 (2) of the Enforcement Rule of the Act on the Management and Use of Information on Commodity Lists;
2. Management data under Article 5 (3) of the Enforcement Rule of the Act on the Management and Use of Information on Commodity Lists.

(2) When publishing an invitation to tender for purchasing fire equipment, requesting submission of a quotation, or concluding a contract, the head of a fire service agency shall obtain the data necessary for listing from the contractor, supplier, manufacturer, etc. under Article 6 (2) of the Enforcement Rule of the Act on the Management and Use of Information on Commodity Lists.

Article 4 (Composition of the Technical Committee)
(1) The Deliberative Committee on Technology of Fire Equipment under Article 8 (1) of the Decree (hereinafter referred to as the “Technical Committee”) shall consist of not more than 60 members, including one chairperson.

(2) The chairperson of the Technical Committee (hereinafter referred to as the “chairperson” in this Article and Article 5) shall be a person designated by the Fire Commissioner of the National Fire Agency from among officials of the National Fire Agency responsible for the field of fire equipment. The members shall be appointed or commissioned by the Fire Commissioner of the National Fire Agency from among persons falling under any of the following:
1. A fire official who is or has been responsible for duties related to fire equipment at the National Fire Agency or a fire service agency and whom the Fire Commissioner of the National Fire Agency deems suitable as a member;
2. A person at or above the rank of principal researcher at a testing or inspection institution accredited by an accrediting body under Article 23 (2) of the Framework Act on National Standards;
3. A person with at least five years of research experience in a relevant field at a school under subparagraphs 1 through 6 of Article 2 of the Higher Education Act;
4. A person with at least ten years of work experience in the relevant technical field at a manufacturer related to fire equipment;
5. Other persons with extensive expertise and experience concerning fire equipment.

(3) The term of office of a member who is not a public official shall be three years, and he or she may be reappointed for one consecutive term only.

Article 5 (Operation of the Technical Committee)
(1) The chairperson shall represent the Technical Committee and exercise overall control over its affairs.

(2) Where the chairperson is unable to perform his or her duties for unavoidable reasons, a member designated in advance by the chairperson shall act on his or her behalf.

(3) The chairperson shall convene meetings of the Technical Committee and preside over them.

(4) A meeting of the Technical Committee shall consist of the chairperson and eight members designated by the chairperson for each meeting.

(5) A majority of the members constituting the meeting under paragraph (4) shall constitute a quorum, and resolutions shall be adopted with the affirmative votes of a majority of the members present.

(6) The Technical Committee shall have one secretary to handle its administrative affairs, and the chairperson shall designate the secretary from among officials of the National Fire Agency.

(7) Members who are not public officials and attend a meeting may be paid allowances, travel expenses and other necessary expenses within budgetary limits.

(8) Matters necessary for operation of the Technical Committee, other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) through (7), shall be determined by the Fire Commissioner of the National Fire Agency.

Article 6 (Exclusion, Challenge and Recusal of Members)
(1) A member of the Technical Committee shall be excluded from its deliberations and resolutions in any of the following cases:
1. Where the member, his or her spouse or former spouse is a party to the relevant matter (including an executive officer where the party is a corporation, organization, etc.; hereinafter the same shall apply in this subparagraph and subparagraph 2), or is a joint holder of rights or joint obligor with a party to that matter;
2. Where the member is or was a relative of a party to the relevant matter;
3. Where the member has given testimony, made a statement, provided advice, conducted research, performed services, or made an appraisal regarding the relevant matter;
4. Where the member or the corporation, organization, etc. to which the member belongs is or was an agent of a party to the relevant matter.

(2) A party to a matter subject to deliberation by the Technical Committee may challenge a member before the Committee where circumstances make it difficult to expect impartial deliberation or resolution by that member. The Committee shall decide on the challenge by resolution. The member subject to the challenge shall not participate in that resolution.

(3) Where a member falls under any ground for exclusion under the subparagraphs of paragraph (1), he or she shall voluntarily recuse himself or herself from deliberation and resolution on the relevant matter.

Article 7 (Dismissal and Decommissioning of Members)
The Fire Commissioner of the National Fire Agency may dismiss a member of the Technical Committee or withdraw his or her commission in any of the following cases:
1. Where the member becomes unable to perform his or her duties due to a mental or physical disability;
2. Where the member has committed misconduct related to his or her duties;
3. Where the member is deemed unsuitable because of neglect of duties, impairment of dignity, or other reasons;
4. Where the member fails to recuse himself or herself despite falling under any subparagraph of Article 6 (1);
5. Where the member personally expresses that it is difficult to perform his or her duties.

Article 8 (Application for Certification)
(1) A manufacturer or seller of fire equipment who intends to obtain certification under Article 12 (2) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as an “applicant for certification”) shall submit an application for certification of fire equipment in attached Form 1 (including an electronic document), together with the following documents (including electronic documents), to a certification institution under Article 14 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a “certification institution”), pursuant to Article 11 of the Decree:
1. A copy of the business registration certificate or an equivalent document (where two or more factories manufacture the same product, one shall be attached for each factory);
2. A document proving agency, such as a power of attorney (only where an agent applies);
3. An explanatory document for the fire equipment;
4. A parts specification list for the fire equipment;
5. Technical documents, such as machinery manufacturing drawings;
6. Data concerning operation of the quality management system, etc.;
7. Where product testing and on-site evaluation are omitted under Article 12 (3) of the Decree, documents proving the grounds therefor.

(2) When applying for certification of fire equipment, the applicant for certification shall submit materials for product testing to the certification institution in the quantity determined and publicly notified by the Fire Commissioner of the National Fire Agency.

Article 9 (Methods of Certification Examination)
(1) Certification examination shall proceed in the order of document screening, product testing and on-site evaluation under Article 12 of the Decree. Product testing and on-site evaluation may proceed simultaneously.

(2) Document screening shall examine whether the fire equipment for which certification is sought falls within fire equipment subject to certification under Article 9 of the Decree and whether it corresponds to the contents of the application and attached documents submitted under Article 8 (1).

(3) Product testing shall examine whether the fire equipment for which certification is sought complies with the product examination standards under subparagraph 1 of Article 10 of the Decree.

(4) On-site evaluation shall examine whether the applicant's manufacturing facilities and testing facilities for fire equipment, their personnel, and the quality management system comply with the on-site evaluation standards under subparagraph 2 of Article 10 of the Decree.

(5) Where documents, materials for product testing, etc. submitted for document screening, product testing and on-site evaluation under paragraphs (1) through (3) need to be supplemented, the head of the certification institution may request the applicant for certification to supplement the application documents or materials for product testing, specifying the matters to be supplemented and the period for supplementation. Where the requested matters are not supplemented by the deadline set by the head of the certification institution, or the applicant for certification voluntarily requests the return of the application documents and materials for product testing, the application documents or materials for product testing may be returned.

Article 10 (Certificate of Fire Equipment)
The certificate of fire equipment under Article 13 of the Decree shall be in attached Form 2.

Article 11 (Application for Designation as a Certification Institution)
(1) A person who intends to be designated as a certification institution under Article 14 (2) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a “certification institution”) shall submit an application for designation as a certification institution for fire equipment in attached Form 3 (including an electronic document), together with the following documents (including electronic documents), to the Fire Commissioner of the National Fire Agency under Article 19 (2) of the Decree. The Fire Commissioner of the National Fire Agency shall verify the certificate of corporate registration or business registration certificate through the joint use of administrative information under Article 36 (1) of the Electronic Government Act. If the applicant does not consent to verification of the business registration certificate, he or she shall be required to attach a copy of that certificate. <Amended on Jan. 7, 2021>
1. The articles of incorporation of the corporation or the rules of the organization;
2. A business plan stating the scope of fire equipment to be certified by the certification institution. The scope of fire equipment to be certified shall be as determined and publicly notified by the Fire Commissioner of the National Fire Agency;
3. Documents proving that the requirements for designation as a certification institution under Article 19 (1) of the Decree are met;
4. Operating regulations concerning procedures and methods, etc. for certification of fire equipment.

(2) Upon receipt of an application for designation under paragraph (1), the Fire Commissioner of the National Fire Agency shall prepare an examination plan, notify the applicant of it, and conduct the examination according to that plan.

(3) The written designation of a certification institution for fire equipment under Article 19 (3) of the Decree shall be in attached Form 4.

(4) Where a matter under Article 19 (3) 2 or 3 of the Decree changes, the head of the certification institution shall submit an application for change of designated particulars of a certification institution for fire equipment in attached Form 5 to the Fire Commissioner of the National Fire Agency. The Fire Commissioner of the National Fire Agency shall verify the certificate of corporate registration or business registration certificate through the joint use of administrative information under Article 36 (1) of the Electronic Government Act. If the applicant does not consent to verification of the business registration certificate, he or she shall be required to attach a copy of that certificate.

(5) Upon receipt of an application for change under paragraph (5), the Fire Commissioner of the National Fire Agency shall verify the facts and reissue the written designation of a certification institution for fire equipment in attached Form 4.

Article 12 (Preparation and Retention of Certification Records)
A certification institution shall prepare records relating to the period from receipt of an application for certification of fire equipment until issuance of the certificate under Article 14 (4) of the Act, and shall retain those records for five years from the date they are prepared (including retention by electronic means).

Article 13 (Application for Confirmation of Exemption from Certification)
(1) Before releasing or selling fire equipment, a manufacturer or seller who intends to obtain exemption from certification under Article 15 of the Act shall submit, for each type of fire equipment, an application for confirmation of exemption in attached Form 6, together with the following documents, to the Korea Fire Institute under Article 14 of the Firefighting Industry Promotion Act (hereinafter referred to as the “Fire Institute”), pursuant to Article 22 (1) of the Decree:
1. A copy of the business registration certificate or an equivalent document (where two or more factories manufacture the same product, one shall be attached for each factory);
2. A document proving agency, such as a power of attorney (only where an agent applies);
3. Documents proving that the equipment falls under any subparagraph of Article 15 of the Act and an explanatory document for the fire equipment.

(2) The written confirmation of exemption under Article 22 (2) of the Decree shall be in attached Form 7.

(3) The method of marking confirmation of exemption under Article 22 (3) of the Decree shall be as specified in paragraph 2 of attached Table 1.

Article 14 (Certification Marking)
The method of certification marking under Article 16 (1) of the Act shall be as specified in paragraph 1 of attached Table 1.

Article 15 (Organization of a Specialized Inspection Team)
Where the head of a fire service agency organizes a specialized inspection team under Article 21 (2) of the Act, the team shall consist of persons falling under any of the following:
1. A person holding a qualification or license related to the fire equipment to be purchased or having extensive experience in related duties;
2. A person at or above researcher level at a testing or inspection institution accredited by an accrediting body under Article 23 (2) of the Framework Act on National Standards;
3. A person with research experience in a relevant field at a school under subparagraphs 1 through 6 of Article 2 of the Higher Education Act;
4. A person currently serving as a fire official who has served for at least three years;
5. Other persons with extensive expertise and experience concerning fire equipment.

Article 16 (Standards for Holdings of Fire Equipment)
“Standards for holdings of fire equipment prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety” in Article 23 (1) of the Act means the standards for holdings of fire equipment in attached Table 2.

Article 17 (Management Records of Fire Equipment)
(1) The head of a fire service agency shall record and manage the status of fire equipment held by the agency in the fire equipment inventory control register in attached Form 8 under Article 26 (1) of the Act. <Amended on Jan. 7, 2021>

(2) The head of a fire service agency shall record and manage matters concerning management and operation of each item of fire equipment held in accordance with the holding standards under Article 16, using the forms determined by the Fire Commissioner of the National Fire Agency. <Amended on Jan. 7, 2021>

Article 18 (Timing and Methods of Inspection of Fire Equipment)
The timing and methods of inspection of fire equipment under the main clause of Article 36 (1) of the Decree shall be as specified in attached Table 3.

Article 19 (Reporting of Breakdowns and Accidents)
(1) Where fire equipment breaks down or a defect is discovered, a public official who manages fire equipment (hereinafter referred to as a “fire equipment management official”) shall prepare a fire equipment breakdown report in attached Form 21 and report it to the head of the fire service agency under Article 34 (1) of the Act.

(2) Where an accident occurs during operation of fire equipment, the fire equipment management official shall prepare a fire equipment accident report in attached Form 22 and report it to the head of the fire service agency under Article 34 (2) of the Act.

(3) Upon receipt of a report under paragraph (2), the head of the fire service agency shall prepare a fire equipment accident report in attached Form 22 and immediately report it to the Fire Commissioner of the National Fire Agency under Article 34 (3) of the Act if any of the following applies:
1. An accident resulting in a death (including a road traffic accident involving a fire engine);
2. An accident attracting concentrated attention from the media or society;
3. An accident for which an investigation by the investigation team under the latter part of Article 34 (4) of the Act is deemed necessary.

Article 20 (Designation of Fire Equipment Maintenance Centers)
(1) A person who intends to be designated as a fire equipment maintenance center under Article 41 (1) of the Decree shall submit an application for designation as a fire equipment maintenance center in attached Form 23 (including an electronic document), together with the following documents (including electronic documents), to the Fire Commissioner of the National Fire Agency. The Fire Commissioner of the National Fire Agency shall verify the certificate of corporate registration or business registration certificate through the joint use of administrative information under Article 36 (1) of the Electronic Government Act. If the applicant does not consent to verification of the business registration certificate, he or she shall be required to attach a copy of that certificate.
1. The articles of incorporation of the corporation or the rules of the organization;
2. A business plan for the businesses under the subparagraphs of Article 36 (2) of the Act;
3. Documents proving that the requirements for designation as a fire equipment maintenance center under Article 39 of the Decree are met.

(2) Upon receipt of an application for designation under paragraph (1), the Fire Commissioner of the National Fire Agency shall prepare an examination plan, notify the applicant of it, and conduct the examination according to that plan.

(3) The written designation of a fire equipment maintenance center under Article 41 (2) of the Decree shall be in attached Form 24.

Article 20-2 (Designation of Fire Engine Maintenance Business Establishments)
(1) A person who intends to be designated as a fire engine maintenance business establishment under Article 41 (3) of the Decree shall submit an application for designation as a fire engine maintenance business establishment in attached Form 25 (including an electronic document), together with documents proving that the requirements determined and publicly notified by the Fire Commissioner of the National Fire Agency under Article 36 (3) of the Act are met (including electronic documents), to the Special Metropolitan City Mayor, Integrated Special City Mayor, Metropolitan City Mayor, Special Self-Governing City Mayor, Do Governor, or Governor of a Special Self-Governing Province (hereinafter referred to as the “Mayor/Do Governor”). The Mayor/Do Governor shall verify the certificate of corporate registration or business registration certificate through the joint use of administrative information under Article 36 (1) of the Electronic Government Act. If the applicant does not consent to verification of the business registration certificate, he or she shall be required to attach a copy of that certificate. <Amended on July 1, 2026>

(2) The written designation of a fire engine maintenance business establishment under Article 41 (4) of the Decree shall be in attached Form 26.

[This Article Newly Inserted on May 16, 2023]

Article 21 (Fire Equipment Development Competitions)
The Fire Commissioner of the National Fire Agency and the Mayor/Do Governor may hold fire equipment development competitions to encourage creative designs for fire equipment and improve the ability to devise scientific solutions to disaster situations. <Amended on May 16, 2023>

