(b) Marking instructions

1) The certification mark may be proportionally scaled according to the position and structure of the fire equipment.

2) The certification mark shall be clearly visible and shall be affixed, printed, engraved, or otherwise indicated on the surface of the fire equipment or its packaging. It shall not be easily erased or detached.

3) The colors of the certification mark design may differ in consideration of the product, packaging, etc.

4) Where it is difficult to place the certification mark on the surface of the fire equipment, or where a fire service agency or end user purchases a large quantity of fire equipment for direct use and there is no risk of the equipment being circulated on the market, the mark may be placed on each smallest packaging unit of the fire equipment.

2. Method of marking confirmation of exemption from certification of fire equipment

(a) Fire equipment exempted from certification shall bear the marking “Proof of Confirmation of Exemption from Fire Equipment Certification” on the surface of the fire equipment or its packaging.

(b) Paragraph 1 (b) shall apply mutatis mutandis to instructions for marking confirmation of exemption.

■ Enforcement Rule of the Act on Management of Firefighting Equipment [Attached Table 2] <Amended on Jan. 26, 2023>

Standards for Holdings of Fire Equipment (Re: Article 16)

1. Mobile equipment

(a) Mobile equipment shall be held according to the deployment standards in attached Table 1 of the Rules on Standards for Firefighting Resources.

(b) Fire equipment carried on fire engines shall be loaded in a manner suitable for local conditions, without exceeding the maximum loading capacity of each fire engine (meaning the maximum loading capacity under subparagraph 14 of Article 2 of the Rules on Performance and Standards for Motor Vehicles and Motor Vehicle Parts).

2. Fire suppression equipment

Fire suppression equipment shall be held according to standards determined by the Fire Commissioner of the National Fire Agency, taking into account the number of fire engines deployed at fire stations, 119 safety centers, 119 local units, etc., the occurrence of fires, disasters and calamities in each locality, and the characteristics of fire suppression equipment.

3. Measurement equipment

Among measurement equipment, fire investigation and forensic examination equipment shall be held according to the equipment and facility standards in the attached Table of the Enforcement Rule of the Act on Fire Investigation by Fire Services.

4. Protective equipment

Protective equipment shall be held on the basis of individual firefighting personnel, according to standards determined by the Fire Commissioner of the National Fire Agency. Holding standards may, however, be determined on the basis of a fire service office or dispatch unit, depending on the characteristics of the protective equipment.

5. Other fire equipment

The Fire Commissioner of the National Fire Agency shall determine holding standards for other equipment, such as rescue equipment, emergency medical equipment, information and communications equipment, measurement equipment and auxiliary equipment, taking into account the characteristics of each item of fire equipment.

■ Enforcement Rule of the Act on Management of Firefighting Equipment [Attached Table 3] <Amended on May 16, 2023>

Timing and Methods of Inspection of Fire Equipment (Re: Article 18)

1. Regular inspection: Inspection to determine whether fire equipment maintains normal functions shall be conducted according to the following standards:

(a) Fire engines: Inspections shall be conducted using forms determined by the Fire Commissioner of the National Fire Agency under Article 17 (2).

(b) Firefighting vessels: Inspections shall be conducted according to standards determined by the Fire Commissioner of the National Fire Agency and each local government.

(c) Firefighting aircraft: Inspections shall be conducted according to standards prescribed by the Aviation Safety Act.

(d) Other fire equipment

1) Personal protective equipment under Article 29 of the Decree: Inspections shall be conducted daily using attached Form 19 (at each change of shift in the case of shift work under Article 6 of the Fire Officials Service Regulations).

2) Fire equipment other than personal protective equipment under Article 29 of the Decree: Inspections shall be conducted using attached Form 20, as determined by the head of the fire service agency in consideration of the scale of the fire equipment held.

2. Detailed inspection: Inspection to identify physical or functional defects, etc. in the special-device parts of fire engines determined by the Fire Commissioner of the National Fire Agency (hereinafter referred to as “special-device parts”) shall be conducted according to the following standards, starting from the point at which five years have elapsed since acquisition of the relevant fire engine (meaning the starting date for calculation of vehicle age under Article 3 of the Enforcement Decree of the Motor Vehicle Management Act):

(a) Fire pumpers and chemical fire trucks

1) Initial detailed inspection: Within six years from the date of acquisition of the fire engine;

2) Periodic detailed inspection: At least once every two years from the date of the initial detailed inspection.

(b) Fire ladder trucks

1) Initial detailed inspection: Within six years from the date of acquisition of the fire engine;

2) Periodic detailed inspection: At least once each year from the date of the initial detailed inspection.

3. Special inspection: A special inspection shall be conducted where it is deemed necessary for any of the following reasons:

(a) Where the occurrence of particular defects or breakdowns in fire equipment results in reduced performance or a high risk of safety accidents;

(b) Where the fire equipment has not complied with requirements prescribed by individual statutes or regulations, or similar breakdowns or accidents have occurred nationwide;

(c) Other cases where the Fire Commissioner of the National Fire Agency deems a special inspection necessary.

4. Notwithstanding paragraph 2, the head of a fire service agency may omit a detailed inspection or adjust its timing, in consultation with the Fire Commissioner of the National Fire Agency, for fire engines determined to be disused or otherwise not in use.

5. Other details necessary for inspection of fire equipment shall be determined by the Fire Commissioner of the National Fire Agency.

